red By","note":"Isao Takahata was a known admirer of Italian Neorealism. The movement's focus on the harsh realities faced by ordinary people in post-war society, using non-professional actors and real locations, heavily influenced his realistic approach.","direction":"outgoing"},{"source":"aw131","target":"aw230","type":"inspired_by","label":"Inspired By","note":"Scholars frequently connect Takahata's film to Clément's, which also portrays the trauma of war through the eyes of orphaned children who create their own world to cope.","direction":"outgoing"},{"source":"aw131","target":"aw157","type":"inspired_by","label":"Inspired By","note":"Takahata wrote extensively about his admiration for French animator Paul Grimault, whose work demonstrated that animation could tackle complex, mature themes with artistic sophistication.","direction":"outgoing"},{"source":"aw131","target":"aw158","type":"inspired_by","label":"Inspired By","note":"Nakazawa's landmark manga series, depicting the bombing of Hiroshima from a child's perspective, established a precedent in Japanese popular media for unflinchingly portraying the horrors of war on civilians, paving the way for works like Grave of the Fireflies.","direction":"outgoing"}],"lineage":{"influenced_by":["aw131","aw131","aw131","aw131","aw131","aw131","aw131"],"influences":[]},"stats":{"total_connected":7,"influences_count":4,"influenced_count":3,"filter":"all","vault_count":0,"beyond_count":7}}